---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { helpers } from '../../lib/helpers';
const lang = 'en';
const h = helpers();
const { E, P, pc, num, TY } = h;
const focus = h.M('FORD', 'FOCUS');
const f8 = focus.byAge.find((r) => r.age === 8)!;
const s = (k: Parameters<typeof h.src>[0], t?: string) => h.src(k, t);
---
<PageLayout title="MOT Failure Rate Method: DVSA Files, Counts and Limits" description="How MOT Failure Rates counts the DVSA anonymised MOT results: which tests, what counts as a failure, how age and defect groups are measured, what is left out." lang={lang} jsonLd={[articleSchema({ title: 'How the MOT failure rates are counted', description: 'Method', path: route('method', lang), lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path: route('method', lang) }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <header class="mb-6"><p class="kicker">Method</p><h1 class="mt-0 text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">How the MOT failure rates are counted</h1></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <p>Every page and every tool reads the same two files, generated by one script, <code>data/build-mot.py</code>, from the <Fragment set:html={s('dvsa_anon', 'DfT and DVSA anonymised MOT tests and results')} /> for {TY}, published on {displayDate(E.D.meta.published, 'en-GB')} under the <Fragment set:html={s('ogl', 'Open Government Licence v3.0')} />. The {TY} file is the latest DVSA has published: the {TY + 1} and {TY + 2} files did not exist when the data was read on {displayDate(E.D.meta.read, 'en-GB')}.</p>
    <h2>1. Which tests are counted</h2>
    <p>The results file has one line per MOT test. We keep class 4 tests (cars, light vans up to 3,000 kg and passenger vehicles up to 8 seats), of type "normal test", that ended in a pass (P), a fail (F) or a pass after rectification at the station (PRS). Retests, abandoned, aborted and refused tests are left out. That leaves {num(E.D.meta.allTests)} tests; {num(E.D.meta.tests)} remain once vehicles under two years old (mostly taxis and early tests) are set aside for the rates by year.</p>
    <h2>2. What counts as a failure</h2>
    <p>A failure is an initial fail: F or PRS. A car whose defect was put right during the test still failed the test as it was brought in, which is how DVSA defines its "initial fail rate" in the <Fragment set:html={s('dvsa_stats', 'MOT testing data for Great Britain')} />. On that definition, {pc(h.gb.fails / h.gb.tests)} of the {TY} tests failed; DVSA's own tables give {pc(P.national.initialFailRate)} for cars, vans and passenger vehicles up to 12 seats (classes 3 and 4) in {P.national.yearLabel} and {pc(P.national.q1InitialFailRate)} in {P.national.q1Label}.</p>
    <h2>3. Age</h2>
    <p>Age is counted from the year of first use recorded in the file: a car first used in 2015 is "8 years old" in the {TY} tests. Example: Ford Focus cars first registered in {f8.year} took {num(f8.n)} tests in {TY} and {pc(f8.r)} failed, against {pc(f8.nat)} for all cars of that year. The defect groups by age use the age in completed years at the test date, which is why the two can differ by one year around a birthday.</p>
    <h2>4. Make and model</h2>
    <p>Make and model are written by the tester or taken from the DVLA record, and DVSA publishes them as they are. A model is shown when it has at least 10,000 tests; a rate at one age, when it rests on at least 300 tests (100 in the checker, which shows the count). Some models appear under two names: MINI cars are split between "Mini" and "Cooper", so no Mini model page is published.</p>
    <h2>5. Defect groups and failure items</h2>
    <p>The failure item file lists every defect recorded on a test. We join the items of type F and P to the failed tests of the results file and group them by the sections of the <Fragment set:html={s('gov_manual', 'MOT inspection manual')} />. A group's share is the share of failed tests with at least one failure item in it; a failed test averages {num(P.categories.defectsPerFailure, 2)} items, so shares add up to more than 100%.</p>
    <h2>6. Comparison with cars of the same age</h2>
    <p>Older cars fail more, so a model's own percentage mostly reflects how old its cars are. Each model is therefore set beside a second percentage: the share of tests that would have failed if each of its cars had failed as often as all cars registered the same year. For one registration year, that is simply the rate of all cars of that year. For a model with all its years together, each year counts in proportion to that model's own tests. Example: Ford Focus cars, all years together, failed {pc(focus.rate)} of their tests, against {pc(focus.same)} for all cars of the same ages. Lists that rank models against cars the same age put first the model whose percentage is furthest below the one of all cars of the same ages, in proportion to it; that is why a young model with a low percentage can come after an older one with a higher percentage.</p>
    <h2>7. What is left out</h2>
    <p>Northern Ireland (tested by the DVA), motorcycles, heavy goods vehicles and vans over 3,000 kg. The data says nothing about servicing, previous owners or how far the car will go: it describes cars of the same make, model and age as a group. Fees, dates and rules come from GOV.UK pages read on {displayDate(P.retrieved_at, 'en-GB')}.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-navy-600">Last read on <time datetime={LAST_UPDATED}>{displayDate(LAST_UPDATED, 'en-GB')}</time>. Questions: <a href={route('contact', lang)}>contact</a>.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
